    {
      "id": "chatcmpl-E53F62q2n3tSxh6n0H7gRvrCwwaI7",
      "role": "assistant",
      "created": 1784872812,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "I can’t answer from the EHR data right now because the mandatory MedCP database connection failed twice:\n\n`Adaptive Server connection failed (QCDIDDWDB001.ucsfmedicalcenter.org)`\n\nSo I could not inspect tables or compute 180-day fracture-admission rates for anabolic agents vs oral bisphosphonates. Once MedCP connectivity is restored, I would compare postmenopausal osteoporosis patients initiating:\n\n- anabolic agents: e.g. teriparatide, abaloparatide, romosozumab\n- oral bisphosphonates: e.g. alendronate, risedronate, ibandronate\n\nand measure inpatient admissions with fracture diagnoses within 180 days after treatment start, ideally with adjustment for confounding by indication since anabolic users often have more severe baseline osteoporosis."
        }
      ],
      "metadata": {
        "userVisible": true,
        "agentVisible": true
      }
